1	// Acceptance tests for the change request:
2	//   "Do not really delete a project. Just tuck it away so it drops off my lists. Keep it off the
3	//    main project list and off the list on the client's page. I do not want to lose anything.
4	//    Note it when I do."
5	//
6	// ARCHIVING is a soft hide, NOT a real delete (that is the separate project-delete feature, which
7	// still removes the row for good). Each project in the projects list offers a control to ARCHIVE it
8	// (project-archive-<id>). Archiving a project:
9	//   - drops it off the MAIN projects list (projects-page), leaving its siblings untouched;
10	//   - drops it off the list on the CLIENT'S page (client-projects-table) too;
11	//   - loses NOTHING — a projects-show-archived toggle on the projects page reveals the archived
12	//     projects again, still carrying their names;
13	//   - appends exactly one audit record — PROJECT_ARCHIVED id=<id> — so the act is noted (the UI
14	//     only stops showing the project in the default view; it cannot show that it was archived).
15	//
16	// Asserts ONLY through the two public channels: the UI (data-testid) and the audit file
17	// (auditLines()). Data is arranged via resetAndSeed; the archive is driven through the UI. Seed
18	// honours clients: { id, name, email } and projects: { id, clientId, name, archived } — `archived`
19	// places a project directly in the tucked-away state so the hiding can be checked without a click.
20	//
